 -0.2528901994228363, "top": [[" Intel", -0.2528901994228363], [" Xe", -2.402514934539795]]}, {"t": " Arc", "lp": -0.2833222448825836, "top": [[" Arc", -0.2833222448825836], [" ARC", -3.024807929992676]]}, {"t": " Linux", "lp": -0.19247320294380188, "top": [[" Linux", -0.19247320294380188], [" community", -3.007735013961792]]}, {"t": " community", "lp": -0.8200689554214478, "top": [[" community", -0.8200689554214478], [" wiki", -1.9874465465545654]]}, {"t": "\n\n", "lp": -0.2621171772480011, "top": [["\n\n", -0.2621171772480011], [" or", -2.248035192489624]]}, {"t": "Would", "lp": -0.07748203724622726, "top": [["Would", -0.07748203724622726], ["Could", -3.8896844387054443]]}, {"t": " you", "lp": -0.001039687660522759, "top": [[" you", -0.001039687660522759], [" like", -7.978359222412109]]}, {"t": " like", "lp": -0.0075853131711483, "top": [[" like", -0.0075853131711483], [" tell", -5.083421230316162]]}, {"t": " me", "lp": -0.026902198791503906, "top": [[" me", -0.026902198791503906], [" more", -3.833235740661621]]}, {"t": " to", "lp": -0.0007166449795477092, "top": [[" to", -0.0007166449795477092], [" elabor", -8.340394020080566]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing / preemption-based**\n- CCS contexts can be scheduled on the same physical contexts as GUC-managed contexts\n- More flexible but can have contention between different context types\n- Generally the default and most compatible option\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Dedicated CCS contexts**\n- CCS contexts get their own dedicated context slots, separate from GUC contexts\n- Reduces contention between CCS and GUC workloads\n- Can improve predictability for compute-heavy workloads\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- **Dedicated CCS contexts with additional isolation**\n- Provides the strongest separation between context types\n- Useful when you want maximum isolation and want to avoid any potential interference between compute (CCS) and graphics/GUC workloads\n\n## When to use each\n\n| Mode | Use case |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | Default, general compatibility, mixed workloads |\n| **2** | Compute-focused workloads wanting dedicated CCS slots |\n| **4** | Workloads needing maximum isolation between CCS and GUC contexts |\n\n---\n\n**A note on accuracy:** I want to be upfront that I'm not fully certain about the precise semantics of these specific mode numbers, as the Xe driver's CCS handling has evolved and the exact definitions can vary between driver versions. The general principle\u2014that higher modes provide more dedicated/isolated handling for CCS contexts\u2014holds, but I'd recommend verifying against:\n\n- Your specific driver version's documentation\n- The kernel source (`drivers/gpu/drm/xe/`)\n- Community resources like the Intel Arc Linux community\n\nWould you like me to"}